      "output": "To solve the inequality $\\frac{1}{x - 5} > 0$, we follow these steps:\n\n### Step 1: Determine the domain\nThe expression is undefined when the denominator is zero. Therefore, we must exclude values of $x$ that make $x - 5 = 0$.\n$$x - 5 \\neq 0 \\implies x \\neq 5$$\nSo, any solution to our inequality must be in the set of real numbers excluding $5$.\n\n### Step 2: Analyze the sign of the fraction\nA rational number $\\frac{a}{b}$ is positive if and only if both the numerator and the denominator are positive ($+$), or both are negative ($-$).\nHere, the numerator is $1$, which is always positive ($+$).\nFor the entire fraction to be greater than $0$ ($>$), the denominator must also be positive.\nThus, we need:\n$$x - 5 > 0$$\n\n### Step 3: Solve the resulting inequality\nSolving $x - 5 > 0$:\nAdd $5$ to both sides:\n$$x > 5$$\n\nThis means all real numbers strictly greater than $5$ will make the fraction positive.\n\n### Step 4: Verify with a test point\nLet's choose a value from our derived interval $(5, \\infty)$, such as $x = 6$.\nSubstitute into the original expression:\n$$\\frac{1}{6 - 5} = \\frac{1}{1} = 1$$\nSince $1 > 0$, this confirms our solution is valid.\n\n### Conclusion\nThe solution set consists of all real numbers greater than $5$. In interval notation, this is written as $(5, \\infty)$.\n\n\\boxed{(5, \\infty)}"
