      "output": "To factor the expression $ab + 5b + 2a + 10$, we will look for common terms and group them to find a perfect square trinomial.\n\n**Step 1: Identify the common factor.**\nAll four terms ($ab$, $5b$, $2a$, and $10$) are divisible by their greatest common divisor, which is $1$. We can write the expression as:\n$$ ab + 5b + 2a + 10 = (ab + 5b) + (2a + 10) $$\n\n**Step 2: Factor by grouping.**\nGroup the first two terms and the last two terms together:\n$$ (ab + 5b) + (2a + 10) $$\n\nNow, factor out the greatest common factor from each group:\n- From $(ab + 5b)$, factor out $b$:\n  $$ b(a + 5) $$\n- From $(2a + 10)$, factor out $2$:\n  $$ 2(a + 5) $$\n\nSo the expression becomes:\n$$ b(a + 5) + 2(a + 5) $$\n\n**Step 3: Combine like terms.**\nSince both groups contain the common binomial factor $(a + 5)$, we can combine them into a single factored term:\n$$ (a + 5)(b + 2) $$\n\nAlternatively, this could be written as $(b + 2)(a + 5)$. Both forms are correct.\n\n\\boxed{(a+5)(b+2)}"
